# Oriel 0.8.0 · October 4, 2026

Oriel 0.8.0 introduces the optional, experimental native UI renderer alongside
the default system WebView. The same app HTML, CSS and JavaScript run in
QuickJS and draw through Oriel's native platform backends.

## Added

- Build with `-Dnative_ui` on Linux, Windows, macOS, Android and iOS.
- `oriel android dev/build` and `oriel ios dev/build` forward Zig `-D` options.
  For example: `oriel android build --abi arm64 --apk -Dnative_ui
  -Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.
- Android projects for the Breakout, Canvas Demo and render-bench examples.
- Power sampling instructions and support for Android battery readings and
  Linux battery or RAPL counters.

## Improved

- Android suspends native animation frames while a window is hidden and resumes
  them when it becomes visible.
- Android text metrics, controls, scrolling, input and slider endpoints more
  closely match the platform WebView.
- Native canvas recording and transform updates do less per-frame work.
- Inline text, SVG styling, line layout, gradients and DOM cleanup have been
  improved across platform backends.
- Breakout bounds long-running ball speed, and its Zig mode applies the ball
  count selected in Settings.

## Performance notes

The Android x86_64 emulator completed the render bench at about 60 fps for
animation and canvas workloads. This validates that build; it is not an
Android before/after comparison and does not measure battery savings. The
desktop QuickJS dispatch changes reduced isolated callback overhead, while
the full desktop comparison showed no clear overall speedup. See the
[benchmark results](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/render-bench/results).

The native renderer remains experimental and supports a subset of browser
HTML, CSS and APIs. The default WebView build remains available.
